EN = {}

EN["index"] = dict(
title_tr=None,
body="""<h2 class="page-title"></h2>
  <p class="info"></br></br></p>
  <p><strong>ilMotorediRicerca</strong> is a collective founded in 2008 by <a class="gold" target="_blank" href="http://www.nicoangiuli.com/"><strong>Nico Angiuli</strong></a>, <strong>Roberto Dell’Orco</strong>, <a class="gold" href="http://atelierquartino.com/" target="_blank"><strong>Matteo Fraterno</strong></a> and <strong>Michele Loiacono</strong>. This site is an archive of the projects carried out by the group.
Their works include <a class="gold" href="/en/che-pesci-prendere"><em><strong>Che pesci prendere</strong></em></a> (2011), in which a temporarily closed fish shop was reused to gather the stories of people with a fish name as a surname; <a class="gold" href="/en/adelfia-fratelli"><em><strong>Aδερφια-Fratelli</strong></em></a> (2009), a collective research project and video performance on the railway built in Greece by Evaristo de Chirico (father of Alberto Savinio and Giorgio de Chirico); <a class="gold" href="/en/oda"><em><strong>Oda, non riesco ad essere infelice</strong></em></a> (2009), an installation at the national theatre of Tirana, Albania, that turns a disused place in the theatre’s courtyard into an open and welcoming public space; <a class="gold" href="/en/albania1e1000"><em><strong>Albania 1e1000</strong></em></a> (2008), an Italian-Albanian magazine based in Turin, developed through an exploratory journey in southern Albania; in <a class="gold" href="/en/sogno-cirio"><em><strong>Il Sogno di Francesco Cirio</strong></em></a> (2013), the tomatoes sold at the Porta Palazzo market in Turin bear, laser-engraved, the words of Francesco Cirio, a pioneer of canned food who began his business at the Turin market. In the short film <a class="gold" href="/en/otnarat"><em><strong>Otnarat. Taranto a futuro inverso</strong></em></a> (2009), the present of the city of Taranto is explored, together with an imaginary future scenario hidden beneath the sculpture in Piazza Fontana. Like a real search engine, physical and not virtual, the collective has explored marginal territories of the contemporary, revealing new perspectives through the reinterpretation of collective memories and imaginaries. ilMotorediRicerca developed its projects in a collaborative way: the ideas realised are the fruit of a dialogic process among the people involved. Each project is specific to the site where it is made and mixes research on the place with the creative process of exchange and knowledge. Formed as a collective based in Puglia, it imagined this land as a platform stretching out into the sea towards Greece and the Balkans.</p>
""")

EN["che-pesci-prendere"] = dict(
title_tr="What fish to catch",
info="2011</br>Fish market at the Sant’Antonio pier, Bari</br>",
body="""<p>A fish shop that gathers the stories of people with a fish name, Bari, a project carried out as part of <em>Amarelarte</em>.</p>

<p>“Che pesci prendere” is a project made by ilmotorediricerca for <a class="gold" href="https://www.artribune.com/mostre-evento-arte/amarelarte/" target="_blank"><strong>Amarelarte</strong></a> 2011.
A fish shop left unused during the two-month biological closure of fishing in the Adriatic was reactivated as a collection point for stories: those of people whose surname is the name of a fish. Adele Dentice with the book “Mare negato”; Vito Sardella, a non-commissioned officer of the Port Authority, and his family’s bond with the sea; Pasquale Persico in a tale between Persia and peaches; Pietro Balena and international maritime shipping; <a class="gold" href="https://www.uldericopesce.it/" target="_blank"><strong>Ulderico Pesce</strong></a>, actor and theatre director, who told us his “Storie di Pesce” (“Fish Stories”), turning the fish shop into a stage set.<br />
<em>With: Roberto dell’Orco, Pia Livia di Tardo, Domenico Fraterno, Matteo Fraterno, Michele Loiacono.<br />
Curated by Bruno Barsanti and Fabrizio Bellomo.<br />
Thanks to: Giuseppe and Giovanni Pupillo, Fabrizio Bellomo and Bruno Barsanti, Benito, Walter Dell’Orco, Arturo Cucciolla.</em></p>
""")

EN["oda"] = dict(
title_tr="Oda, I can’t be unhappy",
info="2009</br>Tirana</br>",
body="""<p>The wish to turn an abandoned public place at the national theatre of Tirana, a sort of small hut, into a new welcoming and informal meeting place for the inauguration of the Tirana Film Festival. This was the idea that animated the project Oda, which means “guest room” in Albanian. The sentence “Oda, non riesco ad essere infelice” (“Oda, I can’t be unhappy”), a paradoxical phrase installed on the roof, overturned the abandoned condition of the small structure and turned it into an improvised set for meetings and interviews, a place for guests to take part and for projects and ideas to be presented. It was here that we presented the project “Albania 1e1000”, issue zero of an Italian-Albanian magazine born from the collaboration with a group of Albanian students in Italy. In 2020 the <a class="gold" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Theatre_(Albania)" target="_blank"><strong>National Theatre of Tirana</strong></a>, an important example of Italian architecture of the 1930s, was unfortunately demolished.</p>

<p><em>With Nico Angiuli, Roberto dell’Orco, Matteo Fraterno, Petros Lekapinos, Matteo Locci, Michele Loiacono, Bennie Meek, Piro Micha, Heldi Pema, Marianna Quaranta, Lorenzo Romito, Panaghiotis Samsarelos, Piero Vereni, Mary Zygouri.<br />
Promoted by: Principi Attivi – Regione Puglia, Tirana International Film Festival, Euro Mediterranean Arts, Naples.<br />
In collaboration with: Stalker/Osservatorio Nomade.</em></p>
""")

EN["adelfia-fratelli"] = dict(
title_tr="Brothers. The sons of Evaristo de Chirico",
info="2009</br>Narrow-gauge railway between Milies and Volos, Pelion, Greece</br>",
body="""<p>Video performance, Volos-Milies, Greece, 19’40”.</p>

<p>At the end of the nineteenth century the engineer <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evaristo_de_Chirico" target="_blank" class="gold">Evaristo de Chirico</a> was commissioned to build the Milies-Volos railway to link the Gulf of Pagasae with Mount Pelion, “…a small narrow-gauge railway that connected that Argonautic port with the interior of Thessaly.” (A. Savinio).
A video performance made on the tracks of Evaristo’s train, dedicated to the landscape of the childhood of two twentieth-century artist brothers, Alberto Savinio and Giorgio de Chirico.</p>

<p><em>A video directed by Matteo Fraterno.<br />
Editing: Nico Angiuli, Martin Kaltner.<br />
The artists in various roles: Sabina D’Angelosante, Roberto dell’Orco, Matteo Fraterno, Zoe Giabouldaki, Petros Lekapinos, Nadia Kalara, Martin Kaltner, Maurizio Papagni, Maria Anna Quaranta, Panaghiotis Samsarelos, Ruggero Savinio, Silvestro Sentiero, Spyros Tsalapatas, Adonis Volanakis, Mary Zygouri.<br />
Promoted by: Principi Attivi – Regione Puglia, Italy; City of Milies, Artemidos, Agria, Greece; Isa and Giorgio de Chirico Foundation, Rome.</em></p>
""")

EN["albania1e1000"] = dict(
title_tr=None,
info="2008</br>Exploration in southern Albania and Torino World Design Capital</br>",
body="""<p>An Italian-Albanian magazine made for <a class="gold" href="http://www.torinogeodesign.net/rivista/" target="_blank"> <strong>Torino Geodesign 2008</strong>.</a></p>

<p>From Abitare 483, June 2008</p>

<p>“Albania1e1000” is issue 0 of a magazine, the arrival point of a process of relationship and listening carried out by the ilmotorediricerca group with the association <a class="gold" href="http://www.interculturatorino.it/glossary/mergimtari-torino/" target="_blank"><strong>Mergimtari of Turin</strong></a>. It takes the form of a geographical map and tells, in two languages, the identities of some Albanians living in Turin and their wish to create a magazine conceived as a space of mediation and as a possibility of intercultural dialogue. In appearance it is a folding map which, once unfolded, allows whoever receives it to detach the whole outline of Albania, so as to see it physically as a shape or as an absence, that is, as a window onto the rest of the map. The reasoning starts from the many commonplaces about Albania and Albanians, commonplaces that hide the fact that of this neighbouring country neither the history, nor the geography, nor the culture is known. Alongside and in support of this project, in this issue 0, there are contributions by some Albanian and Italian intellectuals, nodes of the network built between the two countries.</p>

<p><em>In collaboration with the Mergimtari association. Albania 1e1000 is: Riccardo Albani, Imelda Ali, Nico Angiuli, Saimir Baci, Ilir Butka, Roberto dell’Orco, Pia Livia Di Tardo, Matteo Fraterno, Marco Gigliotti, Joana Gjinaj, Irene Guida, Dafina Ikonomi, Ergys Kastrati, Aurora Kokici, Michele Loiacono, Antonella Marino, Edit Mihali, Lirien Mihali, Tome Muslia, Aldi Pupuleku, Luigi Quaranta, Elona Sako, Ilir Shaholli, Albana Temali, Piero Vereni, Valentina Vetturi.<br />
With the participation of: Artegiovani, Ass. Barriera, Besa editore, Davide Barletti, Pandi Belo, Aksinja Gioia, Raffaele Gorgoni, Eilis Cranitch, Piro Misha, Heldi Pema, Kastriot Ramollari, Father Tomas Samaraj, Filomena Vicchio.</em></p>
""")

EN["otnarat"] = dict(
title_tr="Otnarat. Taranto in reverse future",
info="2009</br>Taranto</br>",
body="""<p>Whether one reaches Taranto from Brindisi, along the Punta Penna bridge, or from Bari, surrounded by the fumes of the steelworks that announce the edge of the city from miles away, one immediately notices a transformed landscape, unlike the usual Apulian one: first the Military Arsenal, built in the mid-nineteenth century, and later Italsider (now Ilva), upend the city of the two seas with two walls, laying hands on Taranto and dictating, within a single century, new human and urban rhythms.</p>

<p>The 1960s were the years of the economic miracle, and for Taranto, as for the rest of the South, big industry represented the hope of a social redemption awaited for decades. The Taranto-born artist and sculptor Nicola Carrino portrayed that hope in those years as chimneys smoking with colour and a people in great ferment; fifty years later, in an unpublished series of drawings (Apocalypse Taranto, 2003, prints on paper), the artist considers that hope to have been betrayed.</p>

<p>From this unacceptable situation was born the documentary project, which wishes to oppose the dictatorship of steel with the tools of the imaginary;</p>

<p>Meeting the maestro Carrino in Rome gave us access to his archive: in one of his performances (Costruttivi Trasformabili, Forum Kunst Rottweil, Germany, 1973, 16mm) Carrino raises a wall made of steel modules; in agreement with the maestro, this and other performative interventions of his are reread and inserted into the documentary.</p>

<p>Years later Carrino was the author of the architectural redevelopment of Piazza Fontana (established at the wish of Charles V), the square that anticipates and introduces the visitor to the old city of Taranto and that becomes the way into Otnarat: a city of the imaginary, of collective action and of the return to hope in another possible Taranto.</p>

<p>In this time of the inverse future, the present is handed back to the people of Taranto, and <a href="https://it.wikipedia.org/wiki/Palazzo_Ulmo" class="gold" target="_blank">Palazzo Ulmo</a> in via Duomo (a historic abandoned building in the heart of the old city) becomes the device from which Matteo Fraterno (relational artist from Naples), Lorenzo Romito (urban art laboratory Stalker/ON), Giorgio de Finis (director of the Casa dell’Architettura in Rome) and Ilmotorediricerca (multidisciplinary collective) intercept the needs and urgencies of the territory, thus developing the structure of the film.</p>

<p><em>Directed by: Nico Angiuli<br />
Screenplay: Nico Angiuli, Roberto dell’Orco, Michele Loiacono<br />
With: Nicola Carrino, Matteo Fraterno, Lorenzo Romito, Angelo Losasso, Aldo Scatigna, Francesco Dracca<br />
Editing: Stefano Muti<br />
Sound: Mauro Boccuni<br />
Music: Angelo Losasso, Francesco Dracca, Aldo Scatigna<br />
Graphic design: Roberto dell’Orco, PiaLivia Di Tardo<br />
Production: In Iride Sfoggio srl<br />
Memory project: <a href="http://www.apuliafilmcommission.it/" class="gold"><strong>Apulia Film Commission</strong></a></em></p>
""")

EN["sogno-cirio"] = dict(
title_tr="The dream of Francesco Cirio",
info="2013</br>Porta Palazzo, Turin</br>",
body="""<p>A project carried out within the residency programme viadellafucina-2013 of <a href="https://www.kaninchenhaus.org/" class="gold" target="_blank">KaninchenHaus Torino</a>.</p>

<p>Words drawn at random from “La Cooperazione in Agricoltura?” (“Cooperation in Agriculture?”) and “Relazione intorno alle colonie penali agricole della Sardegna” (“Report on the agricultural penal colonies of Sardinia”), texts written at the end of the nineteenth century by <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francesco_Cirio" class="gold" target="_blank">Francesco Cirio</a>, a pioneer of canned food, were laser-engraved on San Marzano tomatoes.
Destined to vanish in a jar of tomato sauce, or to become illegible as the fruit rots.</p>

<p><em>A project by Roberto dell’Orco (ilmotorediricerca) and Alessandra Giannandrea.<br />
Curated by Bruno Barsanti.<br />
With the collaboration of: Ragazzi di via Agliè, Marta Scanu, Allan Bay, Gelateria Popolare, Pier Luigi Bassignana.<br />
A project carried out within the residency programme viadellafucina-2013 of KaninchenHaus Torino.</em></p>
""")

EN["maerchen"] = dict(
title_tr="The story of the youth who went forth to learn what fear was",
info="2011</br>Sankt Matthaeus cemetery, Berlin</br>",
body="""<p><strong>A collective performance on a full-moon night in the Sankt Matthaeus cemetery, Berlin.</strong></p>

<p>On the full-moon night of 13 August, in the monumental cemetery of Sankt Matthaeus in Berlin, a group of performers freely interpreted the theme of the transformation from man to animal and back. Inspired by the fairy tales of the Brothers Grimm, who are buried here, and by the idea that the cemetery itself is a place of transformation of the body, the project set out to be an experimental “spatial alchemy”, in which the elements at play were time, place, history and the participants.</p>

<p><em>In collaboration with <a class="gold" href="http://www.efeu-ev.de/" target="_blank">EFEU e.V.</a><br />
With Roberto dell’Orco, Alessandro Guagno, Jule Flierl, Gerhard Moses Hess, Kunle Kuforiji, Harald Pittel, Silvio Talamo.<br />
Thanks to Christiane Frettlöh, Raina Gielge and Ludger Wekenborg.</em></p>
""")

